    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (the project contains a single test class, `AcceptanceTest`, which now passes).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe request set already carried a `sharesHousehold` flag whose only effect was to *skip* the duplicate-household rejection. The new spec requires that when `sharesHousehold` is true and the owner joins an existing same-lastName/same-address household, both owners get a **stable shared `householdId`** that is returned.\n\nChanges made:\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a persisted `household_id` column with getter/setter.\n\n2. **Schema** (`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`, `db/h2/schema.sql`) \u2014 added the `household_id VARCHAR(64)` column to the `owners` table (hsqldb is what the tests use).\n\n3. **OpenAPI** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 added a read-only `householdId` field to the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and the existing MapStruct `OwnerMapper` maps it entity\u2192DTO automatically.\n\n4. **New pipeline step** `AssignHousehold.java` \u2014 when `sharesHousehold` is true and a same-household owner already exists (last name + address compared case-insensitively with collapsed whitespace), it assigns a stable identifier: it reuses an existing household's id if present, otherwise derives one deterministically as a 16-char SHA-256 hex over the normalized last name and address. It sets the id on the new owner and writes it back onto the existing same-household owners so both sides carry it.\n\n5. **Endpoint wiring** (`owners.POST.yml`) \u2014 inserted the `assignHousehold` step (under the `transaction` governance) between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`, so the id is set before the owner is persisted.\n\nThe existing `CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique` already permitted the create when `sharesHousehold` is true, so no change was needed there. Because the id derivation is deterministic, two owners joining the same household independently receive identical `householdId` values \u2014 which the acceptance test verifies.",
